name: first steps
boss: n00b
flavor: You don't know where you are yet.
syntax: $NAME
brief: Three commands answer "where am I, and what is here". pwd prints the
brief: directory you are standing in. ls lists what it contains. cd moves you.
brief: Everything else in this system is those three plus a filter.
brief:
brief: Answers RUN. Type what you would really type -- if it produces the right
brief: output it is right, whether or not it is the phrasing anyone expected.
brief:
brief: $NAME expands to a variable's value -- $HOME is where you live.
---
q: {print|show} the directory you are currently in
a: pwd
cmp: ref
ref: pwd
teach: pwd
hint: print working directory
---
q: {list|show} the contents of the current directory
a: ls
out: server\.log
ref: ls
teach: ls
hint: two letters
---
q: %show% the current directory -- don't skip the hidden dotfiles
a: ls -a
cmp: ref
ref: ls
teach: ls -a
hint: the flag ls describes as "Include names starting with ."
---
q: %list% @dir@ in long format, so you can see sizes and permissions
a: ls -l @dir@
a: ls -l @dir@/
out: ^total
ref: ls
teach: ls -l
hint: the long format flag, then the path
---
q: %list% with sizes in K and M, not raw bytes
a: ls -lh
a: ls -hl
out: ^total
ref: ls
teach: ls -h
hint: two flags -- long format, and the one ls calls "Human readable"
---
q: %list% {newest first|sorted by modification time}
a: ls -lt
a: ls -t
why: fixture-cost
ref: ls
teach: ls -t
hint: sort by time
---
q: %list% one name per line, so you can pipe it on
a: ls -1
out: ^server\.log$
ref: ls
teach: ls -1
hint: the digit one, not the letter l
---
q: {print|echo} the word @word@
a: echo @word@
out: ^@word@$
ref: echo
teach: echo
hint: it prints the word plus a trailing newline
---
q: %show% @word@ without the newline, so more can follow on the same line
a: echo -n @word@
out: @word@
ref: echo
teach: echo -n
hint: n for newline -- the flag suppresses it
---
q: %show% the value of your HOME directory using the HOME variable
a: echo $HOME
a: echo "$HOME"
a: echo ${HOME}
cmp: ref
ref: echo
teach: echo
hint: a dollar sign in front of the name
---
q: {change into @dir@|move your working directory to @dir@|make @dir@ the directory you're standing in}
a: cd @dir@
a: cd @dir@/
a: cd ./@dir@
why: conceptual
ref: cd
teach: cd
hint: just the command and the target -- no flags needed
---
q: {go up one directory|move to the parent directory|step out one level up}
a: cd ..
a: cd ../
why: conceptual
ref: cd
teach: cd
hint: two dots mean "the parent"
---
q: {go back to your home directory, in the shortest way possible|return home using the fewest keystrokes possible|jump back to your home directory without typing its path}
a: cd
why: conceptual
ref: cd
teach: cd
hint: cd with no argument already means home
---
q: %show% the hidden files here, but skip the . and .. clutter
a: ls -A
why: fixture-cost
ref: ls
teach: ls -A
hint: like -a, but leaves out . and ..
---
q: confirm @dir@ exists, without dumping what's inside it
a: ls -d @dir@
out: ^@dir@$
ref: ls
teach: ls -d
hint: stops ls from descending into the directory
---
q: %list% this directory so files, dirs, and executables stand out
a: ls -F
cmp: ref
ref: ls
teach: ls -F
hint: appends a symbol showing each entry's type
---
q: %list% the directory showing inode numbers, to catch hard links
a: ls -i
out: ^ *[0-9]+ [^ ]
ref: ls
teach: ls -i
hint: the number a file is known by at the filesystem level, not its name
---
setup: these files came off another machine
q: %list% in long format with numeric ids, not names
a: ls -ln
out: ^total
ref: ls
teach: ls -n
hint: n modifies -l to print ids as numbers instead of names
---
q: %list% so you can spot which entries are directories, nothing else marked
a: ls -p
cmp: ref
ref: ls
teach: ls -p
hint: like -F but only marks directories, nothing else
---
q: %list% in reverse order
a: ls -r
cmp: ref
ref: ls
teach: ls -r
hint: reverses whatever order ls would otherwise use
---
q: %list% everything under @dir@ recursively
a: ls -R @dir@
cmp: ref
ref: ls
teach: ls -R
hint: capital R -- lowercase r reverses order instead
---
q: %list% largest file first
a: ls -S
cmp: ref
ref: ls
teach: ls -S
hint: sorts by size instead of name
---
q: %list% showing disk space each file here uses, in blocks
a: ls -s
cmp: ref
ref: ls
teach: ls -s
hint: shows size in blocks, not bytes -- pair with -h for human units
---
q: %list% across the screen in columns
a: ls -x
cmp: ref
ref: ls
teach: ls -x
hint: same columns as default, but fills across a row before dropping down
